{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '1,215p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\\documentclass{article}\n     2\t% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n     3\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n     4\t\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n     5\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n     6\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n     7\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n     8\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n     9\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n    10\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n    11\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n    12\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n    13\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n    14\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n    15\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n    16\t}\n    17\t% --- end watermark ---\n    18\t\n    19\t\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n    20\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,longtable,graphicx,subcaption}\n    21\t\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n    22\t\\title{Memorization Without Observed Grokking Across MLP Widths}\n    23\t\\author{}\n    24\t\\newcommand{\\TM}{T_{\\mathrm{M}}}\n    25\t\\newcommand{\\TG}{T_{\\mathrm{G}}}\n    26\t\\newcommand{\\LH}{L_H}\n    27\t\n    28\t\\begin{document}\n    29\t\\maketitle\n    30\t\n    31\t\\begin{abstract}\n    32\tDoes increasing model size change delayed generalization, or only the speed of fitting the training data? We study this distinction in full-batch ReLU multilayer perceptrons on modular addition and subtraction. A fixed learning-rate protocol crosses three hidden widths with two AdamW weight-decay settings and three initialization seeds, producing 36 completed training records. Memorization and generalization are defined by separate sustained accuracy thresholds. Within 20,000 updates, 30 records attain memorization but none attains generalization. Wider networks fit earlier without decay and fit where the narrowest networks fail with positive decay; these differences do not establish faster grokking. An audit of the reported endpoint-substituted lag shows that its positive 500-update width contrast, including a degenerate seed interval, follows from fitting times and censoring rather than observed generalization. The evidence supports a finite-horizon separation between width-dependent fitting and unobserved generalization, while leaving the eventual dependence of grokking time on model size unidentified.\n    33\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    34\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/width_lag_contrast]]\n    35\t\\end{abstract}\n    36\t\n    37\t\\section{Introduction}\n    38\t\n    39\tSmall algorithmic datasets offer an unusually transparent setting for investigating generalization: the target rule is known, every possible input can be enumerated, and training performance can be separated from performance on unseen inputs. Yet fitting such data need not reveal when a network learns the underlying operation. Grokking names the striking case in which generalization emerges long after the training data have been fitted \\citep{Power2022GrokkingGB}. This separation raises a basic question about scale: does a larger network change the arrival of generalization, or simply change the point from which the delay is measured?\n    40\t\n    41\tThe distinction matters because a delay is a difference between event times. Earlier memorization can increase a measured delay even if generalization is unchanged. Conversely, replacing an unobserved generalization event with the training endpoint can produce an apparently precise contrast without any generalization event having occurred. A study of model size therefore needs to report fitting, generalization, and non-attainment separately, rather than infer one from another. Training accuracy alone is particularly inadequate as a proxy for learning an algorithm; the broader distinction between finite-sample fitting and useful generalization is well established \\citep{Zhang2017Rethinking}.\n    42\t\n    43\tWe examine a controlled width-by-decay grid for a single fixed optimization recipe on addition and subtraction modulo 97. The models are one-hidden-layer ReLU networks with concatenated one-hot inputs. Hidden width varies over 16, 64, and 256; all other architectural choices are fixed. Two decay settings and three initialization seeds give 36 training records. Our working explanation is deliberately limited: width may remove a fitting bottleneck without making generalization observable within the same computational horizon. A stronger claim, that width changes an eventual generalization clock, requires events that this study does not observe.\n    44\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    45\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L656-L660]]\n    46\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L789-L871]]\n    47\t\n    48\tThe results make this separation concrete. All wider networks attain sustained memorization at the first noninitial checkpoint. Narrow networks fit later without decay and do not attain memorization with positive decay. Nevertheless, no condition reaches sustained held-out accuracy of 95\\% within 20,000 updates. Thus, the observed size dependence is a fitting result, not an estimate of the size dependence of grokking time.\n    49\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    50\t\n    51\tOur contributions are an explicit finite-horizon width comparison across related modular operations, a complete seed-level account of fitting and generalization non-attainment, and an algebraic audit of an endpoint-substituted lag statistic. We show why a nonzero width contrast and a zero-width seed interval can coexist with no observed generalization. These findings delimit what the evaluated configuration establishes; they neither rule out later grokking nor choose among proposed neural mechanisms.\n    52\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/width_lag_ci_low]]\n    53\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/width_lag_ci_high]]\n    54\t\n    55\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n    56\t  \\centering\n    57\t  \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/study_overview.png}\n    58\t  \\caption{Study design and observation boundary. Addition and subtraction modulo 97 are evaluated under a shared fixed-rate MLP protocol crossing three hidden widths, two weight-decay settings, and three initialization seeds. Memorization requires training accuracy of at least 99\\%; generalization requires held-out accuracy of at least 95\\%, each for three consecutive checkpoints. Non-attainment at 20,000 updates is not a measured generalization delay. The diagram summarizes the design, not a proposed neural circuit.}\n    59\t  \\label{fig:overview}\n    60\t  % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    61\t  % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L721-L743]]\n    62\t\\end{figure}\n    63\t\n    64\t\\section{Related Work}\n    65\t\n    66\t\\paragraph{Grokking and candidate explanations.}\n    67\t\\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} establish delayed generalization on small algorithmically generated datasets and describe its dependence on dataset size. Our experiment uses this setting to distinguish finite-budget fitting from generalization-event timing; it is not a replication of a particular delayed-generalization trajectory. \\citet{Liu2023Omnigrok} relate grokking to training and test loss landscapes as a function of weight norm and demonstrate its presence beyond algorithmic data. \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} propose competition between faster memorizing solutions and more norm-efficient generalizing solutions. \\citet{Kumar2024LazyRich} analyze a transition from lazy to feature-learning dynamics, including a two-layer polynomial-regression setting. These explanations motivate keeping optimization, regularization, and representation effects distinct. Our recorded norms and losses do not measure circuit efficiency or a lazy-to-rich transition, and cannot discriminate these accounts.\n    68\t\n    69\t\\paragraph{Capacity and generalization need not move together.}\n    70\t\\citet{Zhang2017Rethinking} demonstrate that fitting training labels and explaining generalization are different problems. \\citet{Nakkiran2020DoubleDescent} show that increasing model size or training duration need not yield monotone test performance. \\citet{Davies2023Unifying} propose a common pattern-learning-speed perspective on grokking and double descent, including variation in capacity. This context cautions against treating a width effect on training accuracy as sufficient evidence about held-out learning. Our grid is too sparse, and its accuracies too uniformly low, to establish a double-descent curve or a general capacity law.\n    71\t\n    72\t\\paragraph{Regularization and the role of the learning rate.}\n    73\tAdamW decouples weight decay from the loss-gradient update \\citep{Loshchilov2017DecoupledWD}. However, its per-update parameter shrinkage still depends on the learning rate used in that update. We therefore compare decay arms under one shared constant rate rather than interpret a change in the learning-rate recipe as a clean intervention on decay alone. The literature discussion is restricted to exact, permitted starter sources; we make no exhaustive priority or novelty claim from this restricted coverage.\n    74\t\n    75\t\\section{Method}\n    76\t\\label{sec:method}\n    77\t\n    78\t\\subsection{Problem, Data, and Evaluation Target}\n    79\t\n    80\tFor a pair $(a,b)\\in\\{0,\\ldots,p-1\\}^2$, the target is either $(a+b)\\bmod p$ or $(a-b)\\bmod p$, with $p=97$. Each operation has $p^2=9,409$ possible ordered pairs. The final evaluation uses a deterministic subset of 4,705 pairs for each operation, with 1,411 training pairs and 3,294 held-out pairs. Thus the training set is approximately 30\\% of the retained subset, not 30\\% of the entire operation table; it covers approximately 15\\% of all possible inputs. Addition and subtraction use independently constructed operation-specific partitions.\n    81\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    82\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L588-L613]]\n    83\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/resources/final/benchmark.json]]\n    84\t\n    85\tThe partition is reproducible without downloading data. Each input and operation are encoded in canonical JSON, normalized, and hashed. Sorting the hashes separates the development subset from the final subset. A second hash ordering with a fixed training prefix assigns the training portion within the final subset. The split is fixed across initialization seeds and widths. Consequently, seed variability measures training-initialization variability on a fixed split, not uncertainty over fresh data partitions. The held-out set is used for recording performance, not for gradient updates. Appendix~\\ref{app:construction} specifies the construction and readable source locations.\n    86\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L588-L631]]\n    87\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L825-L870]]\n    88\t\n    89\tWe report both operations rather than treating subtraction as an unseen transfer task. Each network is independently trained from scratch on its own operation. The shared architecture and optimization recipe permit a condition-specific replication of the width comparison, but not a claim of cross-task representation transfer.\n    90\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    91\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L656-L660]]\n    92\t\n    93\t\\subsection{Working Explanation and Falsifiable Predictions}\n    94\t\n    95\tThe working explanation separates \\emph{fitting capacity under the chosen optimizer} from \\emph{learning a held-out algorithm}. Increasing width can make the finite training table easier to fit without supplying evidence that the network acquires the arithmetic rule. In a regularized setting, the fitting requirement may become more demanding, so that the narrowest network no longer attains the same training criterion even though wider networks do. This is an explanation of operational fitting behavior, not a claim that parameter count alone determines representational capacity.\n    96\t\n    97\tThe corresponding finite-horizon predictions are explicit. At fixed decay, wider networks should attain the training criterion no later than the narrowest model, or attain it where the narrowest does not. If increased width is sufficient to reveal delayed generalization in this protocol, at least one wider condition should additionally cross the held-out criterion within the budget. The former prediction can be checked using fitting events; the latter can be falsified for this particular horizon. An eventual equality, ordering, or equivalence of generalization times cannot be tested unless those times are observed or otherwise bounded more informatively. These are interpretive predictions for the retained design, not a claim of preregistration or an independent confirmatory test.\n    98\t\n    99\t\\subsection{Width and Decay Comparisons}\n   100\t\n   101\tThe input vector is $x=[e_a;e_b]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{194}$, a concatenation of two one-hot encodings. The classifier is\n   102\t\\begin{equation}\n   103\t f_\\theta(a,b)=W_2\\operatorname{ReLU}(W_1x+b_1)+b_2\\in\\mathbb{R}^{97}.\n   104\t\\end{equation}\n   105\tThe hidden width $w\\in\\{16,64,256\\}$ gives $292w+97$ trainable parameters: 4,769, 18,785, and 74,849, respectively. Both linear layers have biases. The implementation uses the default PyTorch linear-layer initialization without a width-specific logit-scale calibration. Therefore, the comparison holds the layer family fixed but does not isolate width from all initialization-scale effects.\n   106\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   107\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L802-L850]]\n   108\t\n   109\tEach condition minimizes full-batch cross-entropy with AdamW. The fixed learning rate is $\\eta=0.01$ throughout training; weight decay is $\\lambda\\in\\{0,1\\}$, with optimizer coefficients $(0.9,0.999)$ and numerical constant $10^{-8}$. All model parameters are passed to the same optimizer without separate bias exclusions. The recipe controller exposes only a bounded open-loop schedule and receives no training metrics. Its evaluated constant-rate setting is identical across operations, widths, decays, and seeds. The frozen controller and evaluator, rather than proposal descriptions, define the implemented method.\n   110\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/controller/src/program.py#L46-L78]]\n   111\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L842-L870]]\n   112\t\n   113\tFor constant $\\eta$, the multiplicative shrinkage contribution of AdamW alone is $(1-\\eta\\lambda)^t$ after $t$ updates. This algebra explains why changing a learning-rate recipe would change both gradient updates and accumulated shrinkage. It does not predict the norm or accuracy of the trained network, whose gradient contributions are not removed. We do not compare the fixed recipe empirically against the original lower-rate configuration because no matched final evaluation of that configuration is retained. Nor do we reinterpret development scores as final-task measurements.\n   114\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/controller/src/EXPERIMENT_SCOPE.md]]\n   115\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L846-L870]]\n   116\t\n   117\t\\subsection{Training Protocol and Sustained Events}\n   118\t\n   119\tThe evaluation crosses two operations, three widths, two decay values, and seeds 1009, 2027, and 3037. Training runs for $H=20,000$ full-batch updates, with an initial measurement and measurements every 500 updates. The worker uses deterministic PyTorch algorithms, one compute thread, and one interoperation thread. Each condition resets the model and optimizer; no weights are transferred between conditions. The environment specifies NumPy 2.1.3 and PyTorch 2.5.1+cpu. Source evidence, dependencies, and output-to-script mappings are included with the supplementary material.\n   120\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   121\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L789-L799]]\n   122\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/requirements.txt]]\n   123\t\n   124\tLet $A_{\\mathrm{tr}}(t)$ and $A_{\\mathrm{ho}}(t)$ denote checkpoint accuracies. We define $\\TM$ as the first checkpoint whose training accuracy and next two checkpoint accuracies all reach 99\\%. Likewise, $\\TG$ uses a held-out threshold of 95\\%. A recorded crossing is timestamped at the start of the sustained sequence, although it is confirmed only after observing the following checkpoints. Events near the endpoint without sufficient confirming measurements do not satisfy the rule. These operational event times are checkpoint-resolved, not continuous measurements of the underlying transition.\n   125\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L721-L743]]\n   126\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   127\t\n   128\tLate-window accuracy averages the final five checkpoints, from 18,000 through 20,000 updates. It is a descriptive endpoint summary and is not interchangeable with either sustained-event criterion. We preserve all completed records, including fitting failures and held-out non-attainment. All 36 records complete, so the results contain neither missing trajectories nor a denominator restricted to fitting-capable models. No new training is performed for this paper; the figures and tables recompute summaries from the sealed final observations.\n   129\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   130\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/completion_fraction]]\n   131\t\n   132\t\\subsection{Capped Lag and Statistical Reporting}\n   133\t\\label{sec:lag}\n   134\t\n   135\tTo audit the retained lag summary, we reproduce its exact definition:\n   136\t\\begin{equation}\n   137\t\\LH=\\begin{cases}\n   138\tH,&\\TM\\text{ unobserved},\\\\\n   139\t\\max\\{0,\\min(\\TG,H)-\\TM\\},&\\TM\\text{ observed},\n   140\t\\end{cases}\n   141\t\\label{eq:lag}\n   142\t\\end{equation}\n   143\twhere an unobserved $\\TG$ is replaced by $H$. This is an administratively capped statistic, not an uncensored grokking time. In particular, assigning $H$ when memorization is unobserved does not define a genuine post-memorization waiting time. When memorization is observed but generalization is not, $H-\\TM$ is the observed waiting period without an attained event.\n   144\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L728-L743]]\n   145\t\n   146\tFor seed $s$ and decay $\\lambda$, the paired extreme-width contrast averages over operations:\n   147\t\\begin{equation}\n   148\tD_s(\\lambda)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sum_{o\\in\\{+, -\\}}\n   149\t\\big[\\LH(o,16,\\lambda,s)-\\LH(o,256,\\lambda,s)\\big].\n   150\t\\label{eq:contrast}\n   151\t\\end{equation}\n   152\tWe reproduce the supplied mean and Student-$t$ interval across the three paired seed contrasts, as well as the difference of mean contrasts between decay arms. All individual seeds are shown in the plots, and descriptive means and sample standard deviations are used where appropriate. The checkpoints, operations, and decay arms are not treated as independent replications. With no generalization events, we do not estimate a distribution of eventual generalization times or infer a population event probability from the pooled 36 conditions.\n   153\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L746-L773]]\n   154\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   155\t\n   156\t\\section{Results}\n   157\t\n   158\t\\subsection{Width Changes Fitting, but Generalization Is Not Observed}\n   159\t\n   160\tTable~\\ref{tab:events} reports every condition's seed-specific memorization times. With zero decay, all networks memorize: width 16 reaches the sustained criterion at 1,000--1,500 updates, whereas widths 64 and 256 do so at 500 updates in both operations and every seed. The wider conditions are indistinguishable at this checkpoint resolution. Their common event timestamp means they satisfy the rule from the first noninitial checkpoint onward, not that their underlying fitting transitions occur at exactly the same update.\n   161\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   162\t\n   163\tPositive decay changes the narrowest condition qualitatively. All six width-16 records fail to attain sustained memorization by the horizon; every width-64 and width-256 record still attains it at 500 updates. Across the entire grid, 30 of 36 records memorize, but no record attains sustained generalization. There are therefore no observed delayed-generalization events, including among the 30 fitting-capable records. The completed evaluation supports a negative finite-horizon result, rather than a comparison of observed grokking times.\n   164\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   165\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/delayed_fraction]]\n   166\t\n   167\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   168\t  \\centering\n   169\t  \\small\n   170\t  \\input{tables/events.tex}\n   171\t  \\caption{Sustained memorization times in updates, with generalization attainment shown separately. A dash denotes no sustained memorization by 20,000 updates, not a missing or unfinished record. The final column counts observed sustained generalization events across the three seeds. Every condition has a complete trajectory.}\n   172\t  \\label{tab:events}\n   173\t  % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   174\t\\end{table}\n   175\t\n   176\tFigure~\\ref{fig:outcomes} complements the event table with late-window accuracies. Without decay, all training accuracies average 100\\%. With positive decay, the width-16 averages are 56.664\\% for addition and 55.625\\% for subtraction; all wider conditions average 100\\%. Thus the narrow-model non-attainment is not merely a transient miss of a threshold close to perfect fitting. It is accompanied by much lower training accuracy in the recorded late window.\n   177\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   178\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_results.json#/conditions]]\n   179\t\n   180\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   181\t  \\centering\n   182\t  \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/width_decay_outcomes.pdf}\n   183\t  \\caption{Late-window fitting and held-out accuracy separate sharply. Panels (a,b) show training accuracy; (c,d) show held-out accuracy, with a separate shared scale for each row. Values average the final five checkpoints. Colors encode decay; circles, squares, and triangles identify seeds 1009, 2027, and 3037, respectively. Short horizontal bars are condition means, not uncertainty intervals. The training axis is restricted to expose the narrow positive-decay fitting deficit; held-out accuracy remains far below the sustained generalization threshold in every condition.}\n   184\t  \\label{fig:outcomes}\n   185\t  % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   186\t\\end{figure}\n   187\t\n   188\t\\subsection{Held-Out Accuracy Does Not Track Earlier Fitting}\n   189\t\n   190\tThe zero-decay width-16 models have mean late-window held-out accuracies of $0.482\\pm0.061$\\% for addition and $0.407\\pm0.006$\\% for subtraction, where the uncertainty shown is the sample standard deviation across initialization seeds. At width 64 these means fall to 0.047\\% and 0.026\\%, respectively; at width 256 they are zero in all seeds. These measurements contradict a monotone improvement in endpoint accuracy with width for this decay arm. They do not establish a general law that larger models generalize worse.\n   191\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   192\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_results.json#/conditions]]\n   193\t\n   194\tWith positive decay, mean held-out accuracies for widths 16, 64, and 256 are 0.219\\%, 0.091\\%, and 0.091\\% on addition, and 0.178\\%, 0.053\\%, and 0.117\\% on subtraction. The variation is not a common monotone width trend across arms. More importantly, none of these small differences approaches the generalization criterion. The evidence supports a distinction between fitting attainment and useful held-out learning, not a superiority claim for a particular width. Full seed values appear in the supplement rather than being hidden behind condition averages.\n   195\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   196\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_results.json#/conditions]]\n   197\t\n   198\tThe mean late-window held-out accuracy over all conditions is 0.142515\\%. The stored reference predictors average 0.865209\\% for a training-label-mode predictor, 0.865209\\% for a lookup-with-mode-fallback predictor, 0.949201\\% for label-deranged prediction alignment, and 1.050732\\% for the initialized network. The trained networks' mean gap to these four references is $-0.790073$ percentage points. This is a descriptive comparison to these particular references, not a test against independent random-label training. The lookup reference necessarily duplicates the mode predictor on disjoint held-out input pairs, so the reference average does not represent four independent controls.\n   199\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/control_gap]]\n   200\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   201\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L805-L847]]\n   202\t\n   203\t\\subsection{Loss and Norm Diagnostics Qualify the Accuracy Comparison}\n   204\t\n   205\tEndpoint loss provides a complementary view of the poor held-out predictions. On addition at width 16, mean final-checkpoint held-out cross-entropy is 339.459 without decay and 8.390 with positive decay. At width 256, the corresponding values are 89.877 and 8.627. Thus, a decay setting can substantially reduce held-out cross-entropy without producing sustained generalization, and its ranking by loss need not match its ranking by top-one accuracy. These are comparisons between the evaluated configurations, not evidence that reduced loss must precede an eventual transition. The full endpoint table is provided in the supplement.\n   206\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   207\t% @evidence[[analysis/derived_results.json#/records]]\n   208\t\n   209\tThe initial parameter norm also differs across widths: averaging the three seeds gives 6.288, 7.383, and 10.904 for widths 16, 64, and 256. These norms are the same across operations and decay arms at a fixed width and seed, because initialization is reset before each condition. This check supports comparability of decay arms at initialization while making clear that widths are not norm-matched. An initial norm difference is not itself a difference in functional logit scale, and neither quantity establishes a particular learning regime. The retained diagnostics therefore sharpen the implementation boundary without resolving the mechanism.\n   210\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   211\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L805-L850]]\n   212\t\n   213\t\\subsection{A Precise Capped-Lag Contrast Without Generalization Events}\n   214\t\n   215\tFigure~\\ref{fig:censoring} shows the capped statistic rather than labeling it as observed delay. Since all generalization events are unobserved, every fitted record has $\\LH=H-\\TM$. Under positive decay, width 16 receives the special non-memorization value 20,000, while width 256 receives $20,000-500=19,500$. Their paired contrast is therefore 500 updates for every seed and both operations. The supplied three-seed interval is $[500,500]$ updates because these recorded contrasts have zero sample variation. Its degeneracy reflects the endpoint rule and checkpoint-aligned fitting events, not precise knowledge of generalization time.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
