g AdamW decay arms and shared constant versus exponential learning-rate schedules.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Concatenated one-hot input pairs and a two-layer ReLU MLP with hidden widths 16, 64 and 256\",\n    \"Full-batch AdamW with weight decay 0 or 1\",\n    \"Shared seed-independent constant learning rate 0.01 versus exponential schedules with the same initial rate and decay factors 0.5 or 0.1\",\n    \"Sustained train/heldout accuracy crossings with a finite 20000-update observation horizon; checkpoint losses, weight norms and prediction controls\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"Original grokking on small algorithmic datasets\",\n    \"Weight-norm and loss-landscape explanations of grokking\",\n    \"Circuit-efficiency explanations of grokking, as untested mechanistic comparators\",\n    \"Lazy-to-rich training dynamics, as an untested two-layer-network comparator\",\n    \"Grokking and model-size or epoch-wise double descent, without asserting an observed double-descent curve\",\n    \"Training-set fitting versus generalization and explicit regularization\",\n    \"Decoupled weight decay and its learning-rate-dependent cumulative shrinkage\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"Synthetic modular addition and subtraction modulo 97; each operation has 9409 possible ordered input pairs\",\n    \"Hash-partitioned search and final subsets; each final operation has 1411 training pairs and 3294 heldout pairs, not a standard named benchmark split\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n    \"Recorded final traces contain zero sustained heldout-accuracy crossings in 36 cases across three seeds, despite sustained memorization in 30 cases; this is finite-horizon non-observation, not proof that grokking cannot occur\",\n    \"Width affects recorded memorization timing, but with every generalization time censored, the reported lag contrast does not identify a change in generalization time\",\n    \"The constant-rate recipe ranks above both annealed recipes on the recorded search aggregate; no paired significance or causal explanation follows from that ordering\",\n    \"Learning-rate changes also change cumulative AdamW shrinkage, so scheduling and regularization effects are not isolated\",\n    \"Recorded negative control gaps warrant separating training fit from learned heldout algorithmic behavior; the controls are not random-label training experiments\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Grokking: Generalization Beyond Overfitting on Small Algorithmic Datasets\\\" 2201.02177\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Task-provided starter entry 1 defines the relevant small algorithmic/modular-arithmetic setting and delayed-generalization phenomenon. Needed to distinguish that phenomenon from the present recorded absence of generalization crossings within 20000 updates. Do not import its architecture or split into this run.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Decoupled Weight Decay Regularization\\\" 1711.05101\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Task-provided starter entry 11 directly grounds the implemented AdamW optimizer. Both decay arms and all learning-rate recipes alter the update/shrinkage balance; the recipe comparison does not hold cumulative decay fixed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Omnigrok: Grokking Beyond Algorithmic Data\\\" 2210.01117\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Task-provided starter entry 3 supplies the weight-norm/loss-landscape comparator relevant to recorded norm, train-loss and heldout-loss traces. No norm intervention or norm-based causal explanation was established here.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Explaining Grokking Through Circuit Efficiency\\\" 2309.02390\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Task-provided starter entry 4 is an expected alternative explanation for memorization followed by algorithmic generalization. Cite as a mechanistic comparator only: these experiments contain no circuit extraction or circuit-efficiency measurement.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Grokking as the Transition from Lazy to Rich Training Dynamics\\\" 2310.06110\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Task-provided starter entry 5 provides a two-layer-network dynamics comparison appropriate to the fixed two-layer MLP family. Its regression setting and dynamical claims must not be treated as demonstrated for this classification run; no lazy-to-rich transition was measured.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Unifying Grokking and Double Descent\\\" 2303.06173\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Task-provided starter entry 6 links the grokking literature to interpolation-related generalization phenomena. Relevant to separating memorization from delayed generalization when all recorded generalization times are censored, not evidence that this run exhibits double descent.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Deep Double Descent: Where Bigger Models and More Data Hurt\\\" 1912.02292\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Task-provided starter entry 10 anchors the distinct model-size and training-duration comparator expected for a width sweep. Keep separate from the grokking-specific unification paper; three widths and no generalization crossings do not establish a double-descent curve or interpolation threshold.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Understanding Deep Learning Requires Rethinking Generalization\\\" 1611.03530\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Task-provided starter entry 9 contextualizes the observed separation between training-set fit and heldout performance, and the regularization comparison. Its image/random-label experiments are external context, not controls performed in this run.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"Any attempt to identify, retrieve, infer the title/authors/results of, or follow links to the prohibited prior paper or any mirror, abstract, review, presentation, blog, repository, citation, discussion or reproduction of it\",\n    \"Broad discovery queries combining grokking or delayed generalization with model size, width, capacity or scaling; use only the eight exact starter-title/identifier lookups above\",\n    \"Citation-graph expansion, cited-by queries, related-paper/recommendation feeds, survey searches or general web result exploration, even when seeded by an allowed starter paper\",\n    \"Claims or queries asserting observed grokking onset, improved generalization time, a lazy-to-rich transition, circuit discovery, representation emergence or double descent in this run\",\n    \"Queries treating p=31, widths 16/32/64/128, calibrated logits, randomized-label fitting or paired bootstrap equivalence tests from an unexecuted proposal as completed experiments\",\n    \"Transformer/attention/Fourier-circuit architecture comparisons, pretrained representations, cross-architecture transfer or language-model memorization metrics not implemented in the run\",\n    \"Generic optimization, benchmarking, scientific-method, paper-writing or broad learning-rate-schedule searches unsupported by a specific approved source\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\nTitle: Omnigrok: Grokking Beyond Algorithmic Data\nAuthors: Liu, Ziming; Michaud, Eric J.; Tegmark, Max\nSource: https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.01117\n\nAbstract consulted:\nGrokking, the unusual phenomenon for algorithmic datasets where generalization happens long after overfitting the training data, has remained elusive. We aim to understand grokking by analyzing the loss landscapes of neural networks, identifying the mismatch between training and test losses as the cause for grokking. We refer to this as the \"LU mechanism\" because training and test losses (against model weight norm) typically resemble \"L\" and \"U\", respectively. This simple mechanism can nicely explain many aspects of grokking: data size dependence, weight decay dependence, the emergence of representations, etc. Guided by the intuitive picture, we are able to induce grokking on tasks involving images, language and molecules. In the reverse direction, we are able to eliminate grokking for algorithmic datasets. We attribute the dramatic nature of grokking for algorithmic datasets to representation learning.\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n    \"type\": \"evaluation_evidence\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"final.metric.combined_score\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"final.metric.completion_fraction\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"final.metric.control_gap\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"final.metric.correct\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"final.metric.decay_interaction\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"final.metric.delayed_fraction\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"final.metric.restricted_mean_lag\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"final.metric.width_lag_ci_high\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"final.metric.width_lag_ci_low\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"final.metric.width_lag_contrast\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"final.qualification.correct\",\n    \"type\": \"final_qualification\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"final.qualification.outcome\",\n    \"type\": \"final_qualification\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"final.qualification.status\",\n    \"type\": \"final_qualification\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"result.best_any_score\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"result.best_correct_score\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"result.improvement_001\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \